  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 241 AM EDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nMoisture advection within a broad ~25 knot low level jet supporting \nadditional rounds of showers and embedded storms in the vicinity of \na stalled baroclinic zone this morning. Some upper jet support also \nnoted from a shortwave and mid-upper level jet tracking east through \nthe northern Great Lakes aiding in some weak frontogenesis. Heavy \nrainfall rates and the resulting potential for more localized \nflooding the main threat with this round given the anomalously moist \ncolumn and potential for training. Antecedent conditions favor areas \nnear and south of Fort Wayne area for greatest flood threat. Did opt \nto add a row of counties on the northern fringe of the Flood Watch \ngiven radar/model trends. Low clouds and some patchy fog expected \notherwise this morning. \n\nChances for rain diminish this afternoon into this evening as the \nlow level jet weakens and the primary MUCAPE axis shifts off the \nsouthwest. With that said cannot rule the low chance for a few \nwidely scattered showers within the elevated portion of the \nsouthward sinking frontal slope, mainly south of US 30 corridor in \nIN. A low level anticyclone dropping southeast through the \nnorthern/eastern Great Lakes shunts the boundary well south \nlater tonight into Friday with a brief shot of lower humidity \nand dry conditions for the entire forecast area.\n\nAnother wet pattern emerges this weekend as several upper level \nwaves eject east over a persistent upper ridge. The associated lift \nand surges of theta-e should focus several rounds of showers and \nstorms in the vicinity of a baroclinic zone that looks to sink south \ninto the area on Saturday, potentially lingering over the area into \nSunday and Monday. River and lowland flooding may once again become \nan issue for portions of the area.",
